WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.660
Film Courage: I was looking at another talented
screenwriter’s site and her name is Shelley

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.700
Gustavson.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:12.620
And she says in this one article “There
are stories that you figure out and then write.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.580
And then there are stories that you figure
out as you write them.”

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.160
Any thoughts?

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.119
Barrington Smith Seetachitt: Oh…that’s
interesting.

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:24.240
Stories that you figure out and write.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.529
Or stories that you figure out as you write
them…

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:32.760
Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn: I think that’s
true both for my work as a journalist and

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.880
a screenwriter.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:43.620
I can go into an interview believing that
it’s going to go one way and it turns out

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.250
to be an entirely new thing.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:55.690
[This] is a little off topic but I remember
when the event in September happened and I

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:00.660
was supposed to have interviewed John Schneider
at that time.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.969
He was Superman…Barrington Smith Seetachitt:
Father…Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn: He was

00:01:05.969 --> 00:01:06.969
the father of Superman.

00:01:06.969 --> 00:01:07.969
Barrington Smith Seetachitt: Smallville.

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:08.969
Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn: Thank you.

00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:11.240
For the CW show Smallville.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.340
And we had been trying to get this thing [the
interview] together.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.700
We had cancelled and he was out of town.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:25.369
And so I contacted his publicists and she
was like “No, he still wants to do it.”

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:32.320
And it was the day that the President was delivering
the prayer after the event in September.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.130
I was on the freeway and we still hadn’t
heard from one of my cousins who lived in

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.600
New York.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:44.840
And so I’m just like weeping in the car
and it was just so stressful.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:50.869
So I pull myself together, I go to his home,
his wife lets me in.

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:54.749
He’s upstairs watching the prayer.

00:01:54.749 --> 00:02:03.979
I’m trying to get myself composed and he
comes down and I’ve got journalism face

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:10.909
on and I’m like “Hello, how are you?”
and he’s like “Did you know anybody?”

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:17.370
All I kept thinking was ‘We don’t know
where Barbara is’ and I just broke down.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:26.879
So what I thought was just going to be this
innocuous personality profile about this actor

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:31.930
turned into a really interesting revealing
story, not only about the character he was

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:40.090
playing but what justice and what patriotism
and all of these things meant.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:47.650
So a story that was supposed to be this cute
little personality profile about this actor

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:51.260
turned into something deeper and richer.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:56.909
I talked to my editor about whether or not
I should include that I wept on this man’s

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:01.600
white shirt [laughs].

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:09.050
And so it was one of those things that even
now in the work that we’re doing, we had

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:16.940
a certain focus and idea about it and I think
as we’ve gone through this screenwriting

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:22.920
workshop in Switzerland and the experiences
we’ve had there, just stepping back from

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.260
the screenplay for a couple of weeks and then
coming back and just kind of reevaluating

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:36.430
what it is that we’re trying to say and
how are we saying it and are we really affecting

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:41.090
not only good story but are we really saying
something with the way that we’re going.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:48.120
I think it can happen but I think it can definitely
happen both ways.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.920
I was open to it as a journalist because it
was always fun.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:59.019
It’s like a discovery when you have preconceptions
of what your story is but then it develops

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:01.480
into something very different.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:14.159
I think there is something very exciting about
that and something very

00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:20.489
freeing about it if you allow yourself to
open to that, it can go in a direction.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:25.310
Because I think even to a certain extent the
things that we’ve done at least in this

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:32.330
screenplay have moved further from what I
thought my original idea was.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:33.630
But it’s so much better.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:39.790
I’m working on a documentary where I thought
I was going to have a particular story that

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:45.979
was going to go in a particular way and time
and finances (because I don’t have the money

00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:52.289
to continue to generate it) once it’s done.

00:04:52.289 --> 00:04:58.610
I can see it’s already a different story
because there was a particular timeframe on

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:02.360
that story that I thought “Oh, I’m going
to get it done and I’m going to get this

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.920
and the personality is going to be great”
and it didn’t turn out that way, you know

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:07.920
[laughs].

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:16.300
And so looking at how that story can evolve
now it’s going to be a…you know the story

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.729
I wanted to tell was great.

00:05:18.729 --> 00:05:26.259
This story could be even greater because of
not only my experience in time but because

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:33.330
of new circumstances and new developments
and new undertones and things like that.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:44.280
So I think working with something and having
it be what you thought it was going to be

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:50.849
is great but also the discovery of something
new from what you thought you had is also

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:52.380
a wonderful gift.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.210
Barrington Smith Seetachitt: I think…yeah…really
if I’m honest, I’m always figuring it

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.680
out as I write.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:07.240
Even if I go in thinking that I know what
it is, I’m still going to end up figuring

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.240
it out as I go.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.740
Yeah….[laughs].

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:22.370
I have inside-out writing and outside-in writing
and the outside-in will tend to be something

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:25.720
where there is kind of an external story.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.770
And so you’ve got the container a little
bit and you’re figuring out all of the ways

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:40.160
that you’re furnishing and arranging what’s
in that container and generating some new

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.270
stuff.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:47.390
And occasionally I have the luxury of doing
kind of what I call the inside-out where you

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:53.460
just have almost nothing, where you woke up
in the middle of the night with an image in

00:06:53.460 --> 00:07:00.710
your head or just this kind of very fragmentary
thing like a grain of sand and then it’s

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:03.370
like you’re making a narrative stone soup.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:05.680
You’re like “Here’s the pebble…”

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.330
Do you know the story of stone soup?

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:12.050
They go around and get the meat and the carrots
and everything.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:19.849
Then it’s more like they found art by just
pulling all of the things from your life:

00:07:19.849 --> 00:07:25.930
your memories, what’s going on, and you’re
kind of building it out from this little thing

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:31.759
and it becomes something where you really
didn’t have much of an idea of what it was

00:07:31.759 --> 00:07:38.069
going to be at all and so that’s pretty
fun.

00:07:38.069 --> 00:07:43.449
And that would again be an example of figuring
it out as you go.

